\begin{abstract}
% SENTENCE r4.abstract.01
Plectis is a repository-based research environment for retaining mathematical work across sessions and carrying selected results into publication.
% END SENTENCE r4.abstract.01
% SENTENCE r4.abstract.02
The public prototype brings questions, sources, proofs, computations and failed approaches together in eight problem collections.
% END SENTENCE r4.abstract.02
% SENTENCE r4.abstract.03
Its publication workflow records the formal-support status of paper statements and binds explanatory passages to cited sources, while keeping these records distinct.
% END SENTENCE r4.abstract.03
% SENTENCE r4.abstract.04
Researchers and agents choose the work; Python tools retrieve evidence, record Lean probes and regenerate views. Maintainers decide what to adopt, and a journal retains entered contributions and review decisions.
% END SENTENCE r4.abstract.04
% SENTENCE r4.abstract.05
A worked revision shows how an explanation can lose a hypothesis while its formal proofs remain valid.
% END SENTENCE r4.abstract.05
% SENTENCE r4.abstract.06
A historical author-run test rejected \HistoricalRejected{} of \HistoricalEdits{} false edits and accepted one false completion claim. Reader benefit, discovery rate and adoption by independent laboratories remain unmeasured.
% END SENTENCE r4.abstract.06
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
\papersectiontarget{systems-lifecycle}
% SENTENCE r4.intro1.01
Research continues across sessions, contributors and drafts. The next researcher needs more than the latest result: which question remains open, why an approach failed, and what justifies the claims already published. Even a preserved formal proof leaves room for a later explanation to drop a hypothesis or describe an unresolved step as complete. A usable research record must retain these distinctions as the work changes.
% END SENTENCE r4.intro1.01

% SENTENCE r4.intro1.02
Plectis supports this work in a public repository. Questions, prior sources, computations, proofs and unresolved steps remain available alongside proposed contributions and their review. The prototype is a local research environment: a researcher or agent chooses what to investigate and which tools to invoke. Broader autonomous discovery and services for other scientific fields remain proposals (Section~\ref{sec:limits}).
% END SENTENCE r4.intro1.02

% SENTENCE r4.intro1.03
The architecture separates authored records, derived views and adoption decisions (Figure~\ref{fig:lifecycle}). A successful proof probe can remain a session result; a revised explanation can remain a proposal. Acceptance updates the source records, after which builders regenerate their indexes and summaries.
% END SENTENCE r4.intro1.03

% SENTENCE r4.intro2.01
Erd\H{o}s Problems supplies the questions and their public discussion, while Lean and mathlib supply the formal language and library~\cite{erdosproblems,lean4,mathlib}.
% END SENTENCE r4.intro2.01
% SENTENCE r4.intro2.02
Formalisation blueprints link a written argument to named Lean declarations~\cite{leanblueprint,leanarchitect}, and the Equational Theories Project maintains shared problem records~\cite{etp}. Prove2Me combines human-audited missions, source-linked milestones and separately submitted proofs, with intermediate results available for other agents to reuse~\cite[Sections~3--4]{prove2me}.
% END SENTENCE r4.intro2.02

% SENTENCE r4.intro2.03
The question is how to keep a publication aligned with its changing research record. Assertions, formal supports, explanations and acceptance decisions need separate identities and links. Checks detect specified disagreements; preserving the mathematics remains a review task.
% END SENTENCE r4.intro2.03

% SENTENCE r4.intro2.04
Section~\ref{sec:world} describes the architecture; Sections~\ref{sec:example}--\ref{sec:paper} follow an explanation change through checking and review. Contributions, observations and related systems follow. Appendices~\ref{app:repro}--\ref{app:source-map} cover inspection, the worked proof, scope and attribution examples, and references grouped by purpose.
% END SENTENCE r4.intro2.04

% SENTENCE r4.fig.lifecycle
